# Carom — Controls, screens, and menus

This file defines the controls, the game's screens, the menus, and how a player
moves between them. Every figure and every piece of screen copy below carries
the name this specification gives it.

## Controls

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The game reads exactly the eight actions below, each raised by the keys given
beside it:
{{else}}
The controls are registered actions on the `LAYOUT` (`dual-vertical`) touch
layout. Register exactly the eight names in `ACTIONS`, in that order, each bound
to the keys `BINDINGS` gives it:
{{/if}}

| Action | Keys |
| --- | --- |
| `p1-up` | `KeyW` |
| `p1-down` | `KeyS` |
| `p2-up` | `ArrowUp` |
| `p2-down` | `ArrowDown` |
| `confirm` | `Enter`, `Space` |
| `back` | `Escape` |
| `pause` | `KeyP`, `Escape` |
| `mute` | `KeyM` |

These eight actions drive the paddles and the menus. The four movement actions
move the paddles, and the mode specifications under `specs/modes/` state which
paddle each one moves in that mode. Menu navigation below states which screens
read each action, alongside the mouse and touch input the menus take.

## Screens

The game is a small state machine, and `screen` names the state it is in. Each
state has its own screen.

1. `title`, the main menu. It shows the title `TITLE_TEXT` (`CAROM`) and a
   vertical menu of `TITLE_ITEMS` (`SOLO`, `VERSUS`, `HOW TO PLAY`) in that
   order. The item at `menuIndex` is highlighted, drawn distinctly from the
   others. The field furniture (paddles,
{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
   balls, obstacles, net) may show dimmed behind the menu.
{{else}}
   ball, obstacles, net) may show dimmed behind the menu.
{{/if}}
2. `howto`, how to play. It names the controls and describes the spin and
   obstacle mechanics. It also shows a single menu item, index `0`, whose copy
   the build chooses and which the build draws as the other menus draw the item
   at `menuIndex`. Confirming that item returns to the title.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
3. `playing`, the live game: both paddles, the three balls each with its own
   trail, both obstacles, the net, the two scores, and the mode label.
4. `countdown`, the hold before the three balls launch at the start of a match,
   rendered over the live field with the same furniture. It becomes `playing`
   when they launch. A ball that takes its home point again during a rally holds
   and relaunches on its own, leaving the screen on `playing`.
5. `paused`, reachable from `countdown` or `playing`. It offers `PAUSE_ITEMS`
   (`RESUME`, `RESTART`, `QUIT TO MENU`) in that order, with the item at
   `menuIndex` drawn distinctly. The field is visible and frozen behind the pause
   menu.
{{else}}
3. `playing`, the live game: both paddles, the ball with its trail, both
   obstacles, the net, the two scores, and the mode label.
4. `countdown`, the pre-serve hold before the ball is served, rendered over the
   live field with the same furniture. It becomes `playing` when the ball is
   served.
5. `paused`, reachable from `countdown` or `playing`. It offers `PAUSE_ITEMS`
   (`RESUME`, `RESTART`, `QUIT TO MENU`) in that order, with the item at
   `menuIndex` drawn distinctly. The field is visible and frozen behind the pause
   menu.
{{/if}}
6. `matchover`, shown once a match is over. It displays the winning side and the
   final score, and offers `MATCHOVER_ITEMS` (`PLAY AGAIN`, `MENU`) in that
   order, with the item at `menuIndex` drawn distinctly. `specs/balls.md` states
   when a match is over and what reaching this screen sets.

## What advances on each screen

`simTime` accumulates `dt` on every update, whatever the screen. Input is read on
every screen. Beyond that:

| Screen | What advances |
| --- | --- |
| `title`, `howto`, `matchover` | Nothing. |
{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
| `countdown` | Both paddles (input and the AI alike), each ball's hold timer, the balls, the trails. |
| `playing` | Both paddles, each ball's hold timer, the balls, the trails, scoring. |
| `paused` | Nothing. The paddles, the balls, and the hold timers hold their values. |
{{else if (eq variant.slug "gyre")}}
| `countdown` | Both paddles (input and the AI alike), the ball's hold timer, its trail, the obstacle clock. The ball waits at its home point. |
| `playing` | Both paddles, the ball, its trail, the obstacle clock, scoring. |
| `paused` | Nothing. The paddles, the ball, its hold timer, and the obstacle clock hold their values. |
{{else}}
| `countdown` | Both paddles (input and the AI alike), the ball's hold timer, its trail. The ball waits at its home point. |
| `playing` | Both paddles, the ball, its trail, scoring. |
| `paused` | Nothing. The paddles, the ball, and its hold timer hold their values. |
{{/if}}

Each update reads input first, then advances the screen the input left it on. An
update reads the input the screen it began on reads, and a screen an update
reaches takes its first input on the following update.

## Starting a match

`SOLO` and `VERSUS` on the title, `RESTART` on the pause menu, and `PLAY AGAIN`
on the match-over screen all start a match the same way:

- `mode` is set (`solo` or `versus` from the title; the current `mode` from the
  pause and match-over menus).
- `screen = countdown`, `resumeScreen = playing`, and `menuIndex = 0`.
  `titleIndex` keeps its value.
- Both scores are `0` and `winner` is `null`.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
- Every ball is held at its own home point with `holdTimer = HOLD_TIME`, zero
  velocity, zero spin, and an empty trail.
{{else}}
- `receiver = left`, and the ball is held at its home point with
  `holdTimer = HOLD_TIME`, zero velocity, zero spin, and an empty trail.
{{/if}}
- Both paddles have `cy = FIELD_CY` and `vy = 0`.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "gyre")}}
- The obstacle clock is `0` and running.
{{/if}}

## Returning to the title

`QUIT TO MENU` on the pause menu, `MENU` on the match-over screen, leaving the
how-to screen, and `back` on the match-over screen all return to the title. Each
restores every declared field of the state to its title-screen value, except
`titleIndex`, `simTime`, `muted`, `seed`, and `rngState`, which keep their
values, and `menuIndex`, which becomes `titleIndex`.

## Menu navigation

Every menu screen (`title`, `howto`, `paused`, and `matchover`) is driven by the
keyboard, by a mouse, and by touch, over the items that screen shows.

### Keyboard

The menus read the same eight actions a match uses, each read once per frame as
a press edge.

| Screen | Action | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `title` | `p1-up` or `p2-up` | `menuIndex` moves up one, wrapping from `0` to the last item. |
| `title` | `p1-down` or `p2-down` | `menuIndex` moves down one, wrapping from the last item to `0`. |
| `title` | `confirm` | `SOLO` starts a Solo match; `VERSUS` starts a Versus match; `HOW TO PLAY` sets `screen = howto` and `menuIndex = 0`. |
| `title` | `back` | Nothing. |
| `howto` | `confirm` or `back` | Returns to the title. |
| `countdown`, `playing` | `pause` | `resumeScreen` becomes the current screen, `screen = paused`, `menuIndex = 0`. |
| `paused` | `pause` | Resumes: `screen = resumeScreen`. |
| `paused` | `back` | Resumes: `screen = resumeScreen`. |
| `paused` | `p1-up`, `p2-up`, `p1-down`, `p2-down` | Moves `menuIndex` over `PAUSE_ITEMS`, wrapping both ways. |
| `paused` | `confirm` | `RESUME` sets `screen = resumeScreen`; `RESTART` starts a match in the current mode; `QUIT TO MENU` returns to the title. |
| `matchover` | `p1-up`, `p2-up`, `p1-down`, `p2-down` | Moves `menuIndex` over `MATCHOVER_ITEMS`, wrapping both ways. |
| `matchover` | `confirm` | `PLAY AGAIN` starts a match in the current mode; `MENU` returns to the title. |
| `matchover` | `back` | Returns to the title. |
| every screen | `mute` | Toggles the runtime's mute bit, as `specs/audio.md` states. |

`pause` is read on `countdown`, `playing`, and `paused`; `back` is read on
`title`, `howto`, `paused`, and `matchover`. `Escape` raises `pause` and `back`
together on one frame, so a single `Escape` press on `countdown` or `playing`
opens the pause menu and leaves it open, and a single `Escape` press on `paused`
resumes once.

When several of a menu's edges arrive on one frame, up is applied before down,
and movement before `confirm`: a frame carrying both an up edge and a down edge
moves up only, and a frame carrying a movement edge and a `confirm` edge moves
only. On `paused`, `pause` and `back` are read before the menu edges, and a frame
carrying either resumes and does nothing else.

### Pointer and touch

Each menu item occupies a rectangular hit region the build lays out.
`specs/instrumentation.md` states the reading through which the build reports an
item's region in logical units, so the menus keep the layout the build chose.

| Input | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| A pointer moves onto an item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index. |
| A touch contact lands inside an item's region, or travels onto one | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index. |
| A pointer is pressed and released inside one item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index, and that item is confirmed. |
| A touch contact lands and lifts inside one item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index, and that item is confirmed. |

A confirm takes both of its edges inside one item's region: the press and its
release for a pointer, the landing and the lift for a touch contact. Two edges
that fall in different regions, and an edge that falls outside every region,
confirm no item. The item every confirm acts on is the item at `menuIndex`,
whichever input raised it, and the effect is the one the keyboard table gives
`confirm` on that screen.

The pointer and the touch contacts are read once per frame, in the same input
read as the keyboard actions, and are applied after that frame's keyboard edges.
A press and the release that follows it may arrive on one frame, and that frame
confirms. A frame carrying a keyboard movement edge together with a pointer or
touch selection leaves `menuIndex` at the item the pointer or the contact named,
and a frame carrying a keyboard `confirm` edge together with a pointer or touch
confirm confirms the keyboard's item alone. A pointer and a touch contact drive
the menus directly rather than through an action.

### The remembered title selection

Confirming an item on the title menu sets `titleIndex` to that item's index,
from the keyboard, from a pointer, and from a touch contact alike.

## Out of scope

- Network or online multiplayer.
- Gamepad input.
- Destructible obstacles. The obstacles persist for the whole match.
- Persistence of scores or settings between sessions.
